        "math_academy_id": "q-342621",
        "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}",
        "worked_solution": "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\ x+1 & =3{x}^{2}-6x+3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-7x+2 & =0 \\\\ (3x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{\u2032}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{2+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(2) & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
